{"name":"Shagbark Olive","hex":"#645d41","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"shagbark","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A North American hickory species, (Carya ovata) characterized by distinctive, shaggy, exfoliating bark in mature specimens, commonly known as the shagbark hickory.","A West Indian leguminous tree, Pithecellobium micradenium."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shagbark"},{"term":"olive","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tree of species Olea europaea cultivated since ancient times in the Mediterranean for its fruit and the oil obtained from it.","The small oval fruit of this tree, eaten ripe (usually black) or unripe (usually green)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a grayish green color, that of an unripe olive."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/olive"}],"entries":[{"title":"Carya ovata","blocks":[{"text":"Carya ovata, the shagbark hickory, is a common hickory native to eastern North America, with two varieties. The trees can grow to quite a large size but are unreliable in their fruit output. The nut is consumed by wildlife and historically by Native Americans, who also used the wood."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/2021-03-03_10_34_58_Shagbark_Hickory_trunk_within_the_woodlands_along_the_West_Branch_Shabakunk_Creek_in_Ewing_Township%2C_Mercer_County%2C_New_Jersey.jpg/330px-2021-03-03_10_34_58_Shagbark_Hickory_trunk_within_the_woodlands_along_the_West_Branch_Shabakunk_Creek_in_Ewing_Township%2C_Mercer_County%2C_New_Jersey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carya_ovata","wikidata":"Q2601238"},{"title":"Olive","blocks":[{"text":"The cultivated olive is a subspecies of subtropical evergreen tree in the family Oleaceae. Modern cultivars are traced primarily to the Near East, Aegean Sea, and Strait of Gibraltar. Olea europaea is the type species for its genus, Olea, which lends its name to the Oleaceae plant family. The olive fruit is classed botanically as a drupe, similar in structure and function to the cherry or peach. The term oil was originally synonymous with olive oil."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Olivesfromjordan.jpg/330px-Olivesfromjordan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive","wikidata":"Q1621080"},{"title":"Olive (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Olive, also called olive green, is a dark shade of yellowish green, like that of unripe or green olives."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg/330px-Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive_(color)","wikidata":"Q864152"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Carya_ovata_Noyer_blanc.jpg/500px-Carya_ovata_Noyer_blanc.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Carya_ovata_Noyer_blanc.jpg/1280px-Carya_ovata_Noyer_blanc.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Carya_ovata_Noyer_blanc.jpg/500px-Carya_ovata_Noyer_blanc.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Carya ovata fruit","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACarya_ovata_Noyer_blanc.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Carya_ovata_mature_fruit.jpg/500px-Carya_ovata_mature_fruit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Carya_ovata_mature_fruit.jpg/500px-Carya_ovata_mature_fruit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&